1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,520
When we imagine aliens, we usually
imagine something strangely familiar.

2
00:00:04,975 --> 00:00:06,132
Two arms.

3
00:00:06,212 --> 00:00:07,412
Two legs.

4
00:00:07,782 --> 00:00:08,982
A head.

5
00:00:09,185 --> 00:00:10,385
Two eyes.

6
00:00:10,567 --> 00:00:11,767
A mouth.

7
00:00:12,078 --> 00:00:14,818
Maybe they're green. Maybe grey.

8
00:00:15,038 --> 00:00:16,518
Maybe they have a few extra fingers.

9
00:00:16,748 --> 00:00:18,898
But underneath, they're still... people.

10
00:00:19,409 --> 00:00:23,709
Yet evolution on another world would
have no reason to build anything like us.

11
00:00:24,409 --> 00:00:26,869
So what could an alien actually look like?

12
00:00:27,422 --> 00:00:31,462
Here's the thing. We don't have to
invent anything crazy to find out.

13
00:00:31,842 --> 00:00:35,822
Earth has already built creatures
stranger than most science fiction.

14
00:00:36,502 --> 00:00:38,462
We just don't think of them as aliens.

15
00:00:39,783 --> 00:00:41,963
Take a flower. To you, it's yellow.

16
00:00:42,674 --> 00:00:44,954
To a bee, it has a dark target painted in

17
00:00:45,184 --> 00:00:47,534
the middle, pointing
straight at the nectar.

18
00:00:48,548 --> 00:00:50,208
Bees see ultraviolet light.

19
00:00:50,808 --> 00:00:52,148
The pattern is really there.

20
00:00:52,228 --> 00:00:53,828
We simply can't see it.

21
00:00:54,045 --> 00:00:55,925
And that's just one extra colour.

22
00:00:56,714 --> 00:00:59,194
Pit vipers hunt in total darkness.

23
00:00:59,614 --> 00:01:03,054
Two small pits on their face
sense the heat of a mouse,

24
00:01:03,134 --> 00:01:05,834
so they can strike at a
warm body they cannot see.

25
00:01:06,434 --> 00:01:11,514
Sharks feel electricity. Every living
fish gives off a tiny electric field,

26
00:01:11,744 --> 00:01:14,914
and a shark can find a
fish buried under the sand.

27
00:01:15,534 --> 00:01:17,654
Some fish go one step further.

28
00:01:18,114 --> 00:01:21,214
They don't just sense
electricity. They talk with it.

29
00:01:21,734 --> 00:01:26,174
Pulses of electricity for courtship, for
warnings, for "this is my territory".

30
00:01:27,054 --> 00:01:29,774
A whole conversation that
we would never notice.

31
00:01:30,272 --> 00:01:33,252
Migrating birds sense
Earth's magnetic field.

32
00:01:33,432 --> 00:01:35,712
And many bats see with sound.

33
00:01:36,352 --> 00:01:38,372
They shout, and listen for the echo.

34
00:01:39,033 --> 00:01:41,553
Now imagine an alien
that has all of these.

35
00:01:41,633 --> 00:01:43,333
Or none of them.

36
00:01:45,353 --> 00:01:47,973
And colour itself might
not look like ours.

37
00:01:48,534 --> 00:01:51,054
Plants on Earth are green
because chlorophyll soaks

38
00:01:51,284 --> 00:01:54,594
up red and blue light, and
bounces the green back.

39
00:01:55,065 --> 00:01:57,145
But most stars in our
galaxy aren't like the Sun.

40
00:01:57,375 --> 00:02:03,185
They're red dwarfs, smaller, cooler stars
that shine mostly in red and infrared.

41
00:02:03,995 --> 00:02:06,235
Under a sun like that, plants might evolve

42
00:02:06,465 --> 00:02:09,095
to swallow every bit
of light they can get.

43
00:02:09,723 --> 00:02:11,963
Which means their forests could be black.

44
00:02:14,193 --> 00:02:16,053
Then there's the opposite question.

45
00:02:16,133 --> 00:02:20,173
If light isn't useful on a
world, why have eyes at all?

46
00:02:20,534 --> 00:02:22,534
Earth has an answer to that too.

47
00:02:23,234 --> 00:02:27,894
In caves in Mexico, there's a fish
whose ancestors had perfectly good eyes.

48
00:02:28,610 --> 00:02:31,950
After generations in the dark,
its descendants are blind.

49
00:02:32,030 --> 00:02:34,990
Just skin where the eyes once were.

50
00:02:35,578 --> 00:02:38,778
Now picture a world under
kilometres of ice, like

51
00:02:39,008 --> 00:02:41,818
the oceans that may exist
on some moons of Jupiter.

52
00:02:42,607 --> 00:02:45,907
Eyes there would be about as
useful as they are in that cave.

53
00:02:46,507 --> 00:02:49,647
And down there, life might
not need sunlight at all.

54
00:02:49,930 --> 00:02:51,870
At the bottom of our own
ocean, around volcanic

55
00:02:52,100 --> 00:02:55,090
vents, there are worms two metres long.

56
00:02:55,658 --> 00:02:58,338
They have no mouth. No stomach. No gut.

57
00:02:58,696 --> 00:03:01,116
Instead, they carry
bacteria inside their bodies

58
00:03:01,346 --> 00:03:03,896
that turn chemicals
from the vent into food.

59
00:03:04,574 --> 00:03:06,374
An animal that never eats.

60
00:03:07,094 --> 00:03:11,614
It would be hard to design something
more alien than that. And it's here.

61
00:03:14,034 --> 00:03:16,354
Gravity would change the shape too.

62
00:03:16,810 --> 00:03:20,650
Make an animal twice as tall, and
it becomes eight times heavier.

63
00:03:21,330 --> 00:03:23,930
But its bones only get
four times stronger.

64
00:03:24,694 --> 00:03:27,694
That's why an elephant doesn't
look like a big gazelle.

65
00:03:28,310 --> 00:03:31,890
On a heavy world, large animals
would be short and squat.

66
00:03:32,570 --> 00:03:34,230
On a light one, they could be tall and

67
00:03:34,460 --> 00:03:36,810
spindly in a way that
would collapse on Earth.

68
00:03:37,275 --> 00:03:40,735
So far, we've changed the senses,
the colours and the proportions.

69
00:03:41,755 --> 00:03:45,395
But we've kept the basic
plan. A body with a head.

70
00:03:47,435 --> 00:03:49,595
Do aliens even need a head?

71
00:03:50,040 --> 00:03:52,960
An octopus has around five
hundred million neurons.

72
00:03:53,580 --> 00:03:54,920
And most of them aren't in its head.

73
00:03:55,150 --> 00:03:57,360
They're in its arms.

74
00:03:58,076 --> 00:04:01,556
Each arm can taste, touch and
make some of its own decisions.

75
00:04:02,354 --> 00:04:04,614
A starfish has no brain at all.

76
00:04:04,794 --> 00:04:07,754
Just a ring of nerves,
and no front or back.

77
00:04:08,404 --> 00:04:11,877
And then there's this. A slime mould.

78
00:04:11,984 --> 00:04:14,924
A single giant cell with
no neurons whatsoever.

79
00:04:15,672 --> 00:04:20,112
Put it in a maze with food at two
exits, and it finds the shortest path.

80
00:04:20,781 --> 00:04:24,921
Put oat flakes on a map, where
the cities around Tokyo are, and

81
00:04:25,151 --> 00:04:28,541
it grows a network that looks
remarkably like the Tokyo railway.

82
00:04:29,325 --> 00:04:31,185
No brain. No neurons.

83
00:04:31,845 --> 00:04:34,785
And yet it keeps finding
surprisingly efficient routes.

84
00:04:35,182 --> 00:04:38,562
So a head is one
solution. It's not a rule.

85
00:04:40,872 --> 00:04:42,572
Now let's go further.

86
00:04:43,112 --> 00:04:45,612
Do aliens even need to be one individual?

87
00:04:46,210 --> 00:04:48,530
This is a Portuguese man o' war.

88
00:04:48,830 --> 00:04:50,510
It looks like a jellyfish.

89
00:04:51,214 --> 00:04:52,854
It may not be one animal.

90
00:04:53,334 --> 00:04:57,334
It's a colony of many small bodies,
each specialised for a single job.

91
00:04:58,074 --> 00:04:59,854
Floating, stinging, eating.

92
00:05:00,573 --> 00:05:02,693
None of them can survive alone.

93
00:05:03,353 --> 00:05:05,573
Together, they behave like one creature.

94
00:05:06,088 --> 00:05:08,708
So is that one organism? Or hundreds?

95
00:05:09,534 --> 00:05:11,674
Biologists still argue about it.

96
00:05:11,754 --> 00:05:15,534
And an alien intelligence could
be the same kind of puzzle.

97
00:05:18,214 --> 00:05:19,474
Then there's size.

98
00:05:20,094 --> 00:05:24,374
In Utah, there's a forest of around
forty-seven thousand aspen trees.

99
00:05:25,075 --> 00:05:26,835
Except it isn't a forest.

100
00:05:27,595 --> 00:05:31,775
Every one of those trunks grows from the
same root system, with the same genes.

101
00:05:32,576 --> 00:05:34,936
Biologically, it's a single organism.

102
00:05:35,676 --> 00:05:37,516
Tens of thousands of trunks.

103
00:05:37,956 --> 00:05:39,516
One genetic individual.

104
00:05:40,313 --> 00:05:44,613
It's called Pando, and all together
it weighs around six thousand tonnes.

105
00:05:45,380 --> 00:05:47,300
You can walk right through it and never

106
00:05:47,530 --> 00:05:50,200
realise you're inside
a single living thing.

107
00:05:50,545 --> 00:05:53,205
And Pando doesn't even
cover the most ground.

108
00:05:53,825 --> 00:05:56,445
In Oregon, a single honey fungus spreads

109
00:05:56,675 --> 00:05:59,285
underground across nearly
ten square kilometres.

110
00:06:00,179 --> 00:06:02,699
On the surface, you only
see a few mushrooms.

111
00:06:05,039 --> 00:06:06,959
And size changes time.

112
00:06:07,474 --> 00:06:12,274
In America, there's a mayfly whose females
spend less than five minutes as adults.

113
00:06:12,946 --> 00:06:15,906
Five minutes to fly,
mate, lay eggs and die.

114
00:06:16,476 --> 00:06:19,576
Now imagine intelligence
evolving on that clock.

115
00:06:19,963 --> 00:06:25,163
Or the opposite. A giant, slow creature
might take a year to finish a thought.

116
00:06:25,783 --> 00:06:27,843
To it, a human life could be a flicker.

117
00:06:30,203 --> 00:06:32,723
And then there's the
question we rarely ask.

118
00:06:33,343 --> 00:06:35,923
Does life need to stand
on a planet at all?

119
00:06:36,330 --> 00:06:39,870
In 1976, Carl Sagan and Edwin Salpeter

120
00:06:39,950 --> 00:06:42,590
imagined life in the
atmosphere of Jupiter.

121
00:06:43,381 --> 00:06:47,201
Giant gas bags, kilometres
across, drifting in the clouds.

122
00:06:47,661 --> 00:06:50,641
Never touching the ground,
because there isn't one.

123
00:06:51,180 --> 00:06:53,900
Nobody knows if anything like that exists.

124
00:06:54,660 --> 00:06:56,280
It was a thought experiment.

125
00:06:56,760 --> 00:06:59,080
But nobody has shown that it's impossible.

126
00:06:59,160 --> 00:07:03,360
And it shows how many of our assumptions
come from standing on a rock.

127
00:07:06,130 --> 00:07:08,810
Which brings us to the
biggest problem of all.

128
00:07:09,426 --> 00:07:13,346
Imagine landing on another
world. You see rocks. Dust.

129
00:07:14,085 --> 00:07:15,285
Hills.

130
00:07:15,460 --> 00:07:16,660
Nothing moves.

131
00:07:17,380 --> 00:07:19,040
"There's no life here."

132
00:07:19,716 --> 00:07:22,436
But we just saw a forest
that is one living thing.

133
00:07:23,116 --> 00:07:25,216
A colony that acts like an animal.

134
00:07:25,756 --> 00:07:27,956
A cell that finds its way through a maze.

135
00:07:28,754 --> 00:07:30,974
What if that landscape is alive?

136
00:07:31,773 --> 00:07:34,313
What if it thinks once
every thousand years,

137
00:07:34,393 --> 00:07:36,453
through chemistry we've never measured?

138
00:07:37,138 --> 00:07:38,658
We'd be standing in the
middle of an alien...

139
00:07:38,888 --> 00:07:41,338
and see nothing but geology.

140
00:07:44,378 --> 00:07:46,998
So what would an alien actually look like?

141
00:07:47,632 --> 00:07:49,932
The honest answer is, we don't know.

142
00:07:50,329 --> 00:07:52,009
But Earth gives us a hint.

143
00:07:52,749 --> 00:07:54,949
With one chemistry and
one planet, life has

144
00:07:55,179 --> 00:07:57,589
already made creatures
we'd barely recognise.

145
00:07:58,470 --> 00:08:03,010
Another world wouldn't start from our
history, our gravity or our sunlight.

146
00:08:03,486 --> 00:08:07,946
When we imagine an alien, we usually start
with a human... and change the shape.

147
00:08:08,595 --> 00:08:11,935
Maybe the real question
isn't what aliens look like.

148
00:08:12,510 --> 00:08:16,100
Maybe it's whether we'd recognise
one if we were looking right at it.
